                <pb facs="0356" n="244" xml:id="pg244"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7041: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-14T17:00:00+08:00">June 14, 1935—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 14—10:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>159. I am confidentially informed by head of a government ministry that Chiang
                    Kai-shek will probably come to Nanking and may arrive tomorrow. Ho Ying-chin, he
                    states, did not reach Nanking until this afternoon, having stopped over at
                    Paotingfu. He states further that the situation looks a little better today than
                    yesterday but no matter what turn events may take China has already done
                    everything possible to accommodate the Japanese and can do no more.</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Atcheson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
